    fields:  # 5504
      statement: "hypergumbo must not abort with an internal error on a real repository. A RecursionError is indistinguishable to a caller from an analysis that ran and found nothing: the process exits non-zero with an empty stdout, which for verify-claims is the same shape as a clean run that wrote no JSON."  # 5504
      root_cause: "Not yet diagnosed. Observed as 'hypergumbo: internal error: RecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded' with no traceback (the handler suppresses it without --debug). Repo is Go-dominant with 192 generated .pb.go files, so a deep or cyclic structure in a Go-side walk is the first place to look."  # 5504
- op: discuss  # 8155
  at: '2026-08-22T05:02:16Z'  # 8155
  by: agent  # 8155
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8155
  clock: 2  # 8155
  nonce: '8155'  # 8155
  message: "REPRO, observed 2026-08-22 on dev 200e151682 (and on 26276a8430 -- present in both,\nso not introduced by this cycle's changes):\n\n    hypergumbo verify-claims ~/ALL_REPOS/whole_bunch_of_repos/keda \\\n        --claims docs/example-claims/generic-taint-claims.yaml --format json\n\n    stderr: hypergumbo: internal error: RecursionError: maximum recursion depth\n            exceeded\n            This is a bug; re-run with --debug for a full traceback.\n    stdout: 0 bytes\n    exit:   1\n\nFOUND IN PASSING while screening 13 repos for INV-rozob, so the surrounding\ncohort is a useful control: caddy, sops, poetry, mitmproxy, knex, express,\napollo-server, pretix, microservices-demo, containerd, kserve, grafana and\ndash.js all completed. keda is the only crash.\n\nWHY exit 1 + empty stdout IS THE DANGEROUS PART, and why this is filed as an\ninvariant rather than a bug report: verify-claims exits 1 for VIOLATED. A\ncaller running it as a gate (`verify-claims ... || exit 1`) cannot distinguish\n\"claims violated\" from \"the analysis crashed and examined nothing\" -- both are\nexit 1 -- and the JSON consumer gets a zero-byte file rather than a verdict\nlist. That is the same certifying-what-it-did-not-establish failure mode\nINV-javam and INV-fibis were filed for, arriving through the error path instead\nof the verdict path.\n\nNOT YET CAPTURED: the traceback. The handler suppresses it without --debug and\nthe run is expensive (270MB repo, Go-dominant, 192 generated .pb.go files), so\nthe next step is a single `--debug` run to name the recursive frame before\nguessing at a fix."  # 8155
- op: discuss  # 4a8d
  at: '2026-08-22T05:10:00Z'  # 4a8d
  by: agent  # 4a8d
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4a8d
  clock: 3  # 4a8d
  nonce: 4a8d  # 4a8d
  message: "OWNER DIRECTION, 2026-08-22, recorded in the owner's own framing before any\ndiagnosis so it is not lost to a summary:\n\n  \"re INV-gotir, iirc there's a serialized/dynamic programming way to do really\n   deep recursions in python. i don't know if that was the actual issue in this\n   case but if so then thats probably the direction\"\n\nSo: IF the traceback shows a genuine deep-structure recursion (a traversal whose\ndepth scales with the input rather than a cycle), the fix direction is an\nEXPLICIT STACK / worklist rather than raising sys.setrecursionlimit. Raising the\nlimit trades a Python RecursionError for a C-stack segfault and moves the\nfailure from a catchable exception to a hard crash, so it is not a fix.\n\nTHE CONDITIONAL IS PART OF THE DIRECTION, not a hedge to be dropped: the owner\nexplicitly did not claim this is the cause. Two other shapes produce the same\nsymptom and want different fixes --\n  * a CYCLE in a graph walk (a visited-set is the fix; an explicit stack alone\n    would loop forever instead of raising),\n  * unbounded MUTUAL recursion between two passes (needs a depth budget).\nDiagnose before choosing."  # 4a8d
- op: discuss  # df52
  at: '2026-08-22T05:19:52Z'  # df52
  by: agent  # df52
  actor: jgstern_agent  # df52
  clock: 4  # df52
  nonce: df52  # df52
  message: "DIAGNOSED. The owner's conditional resolves YES: a genuine depth-scaling\nrecursion, not a cycle, so the explicit-stack direction applies.\n\nTRACEBACK (--debug on keda, dev 200e151682):\n    cmd_verify_claims -> _build_ddg_for_verify_claims -> build_repo_ddg\n      -> _walk_language -> ddg_build._walk_functions   (ddg_build.py:206)\n         [Previous line repeated 989 more times]\n    RecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded\n\n_walk_functions is a PURE PRE-ORDER traversal with no work after the child loop:\n\n    for child in node.children:\n        _walk_functions(child, ...)\n\nso Python stack depth IS tree-sitter AST depth, one frame per level.\n\nTHE FILE AND THE NUMBER. vendor/go.temporal.io/api/workflowservice/v1/\nrequest_response.pb.go, 725,832 bytes of generated protobuf. Measured AST depth\n**1171** against Python's default limit of 1000. The deepest path is\nsource_file / const_declaration / const_spec / expression_list /\n**binary_expression x1165** / interpreted_string_literal -- ONE const whose\nvalue is a generated string built as \"...\" + \"...\" + \"...\" 1,165 times. Go's\n+ is left-associative, so tree-sitter yields a 1,165-deep left spine. Legal,\nmachine-emitted Go; nothing pathological but its shape.\n\nMINIMAL REPRO, 14,550 bytes, no vendored code needed -- suitable as a fixture:\n    package deep\n    const blob = \"seg0\" +\\n\\t\"seg1\" + ... 1200 terms\n    func Use() string { return blob }\n  -> AST depth 1205 -> RecursionError at ddg_build.py:206. Confirmed live.\n\nCAUTION FOR WHOEVER WRITES THE TEST, because it cost me a wrong intermediate\nconclusion. _DDG_LANGUAGES is EMPTY except for python until the force-imports\ninside _build_ddg_for_verify_claims run (the mechanism cli.py documents at\n~5424). Calling build_repo_ddg directly without importing go_def_use silently\nwalks NOTHING for go and the repro 'passes'. I briefly concluded the crashing\nfile was Python on exactly that evidence -- and keda contains ZERO .py files,\nwhich is what exposed the mistake. Import the def/use module first.\n\nSCOPE: THE GO ANALYZER ITSELF SURVIVES THIS FILE (analyze_go on it alone returns\n3551 symbols / 2876 edges), so this is ddg_build's walk specifically, not a\ntree-sitter or analyzer-wide limit.\n\nIT IS A CLASS, NOT AN INSTANCE. An AST sweep finds **72** self-recursive\nfunctions that recurse inside a loop over node children, across core and all\nthree language packages: the whole-file _extract_*_symbols_recursive /\n_extract_edges_recursive family, _collect_ids_recursive in every def/use\nextractor, cfg.visit, taint_refine, producer_coherence, fallback_coherence,\nblock_hash, fingerprint, sketch.render_tree. Any entered at a file root has the\nsame exposure; the ones walking a bounded subtree (a declarator, a TOML key, a\npattern) do not.\n\nCORPUS AST-DEPTH CENSUS, so the class is sized on a number rather than on the\nsize of the grep. Files >= 60KB, tree-sitter depth measured ITERATIVELY so the\ninstrument cannot hit the bug it measures:\n\n    repo        scanned   depth>=500   depth>=900   worst\n    keda            390            8            7    1171\n    dash.js          20            0            0      74\n    caddy             2            0            0      37\n    mitmproxy         5            0            0      79\n\nAll seven keda offenders are generated Go under vendor/: the protobuf above and\nsix golang.org/x/text/unicode/norm/tables*.go at 947-948 -- WITHIN 5% OF THE\nLIMIT, so this is not a single-file curiosity; a slightly larger Unicode table\ncrashes them too. Hand-written code across three other repos tops out near 80.\nNote _SKIP_DIRS excludes node_modules and .venv but NOT vendor/, which is why\nGo's vendored tree is walked at all.\n\nWHY NOT sys.setrecursionlimit: it trades a catchable RecursionError for a\nC-stack segfault, strictly worse for a tool whose failure here is already 'exit\n1 with empty stdout, indistinguishable from VIOLATED'. The explicit-stack\ntransformation is exactly equivalent for this function BECAUSE there is no work\nafter the children loop: the recursion carries no state a worklist cannot hold,\nand visit order is preserved by pushing children reversed."  # df52
