- op: discuss  # 9d74
  at: '2026-08-22T08:32:05Z'  # 9d74
  by: agent  # 9d74
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9d74
  clock: 6  # 9d74
  nonce: 9d74  # 9d74
  message: "FIXED AND MERGED, PR #478 (dev eba51a0974). Closing on behavioural evidence from\nthe repo that produced the crash, per the closure-evidence discipline.\n\nTHE FIX the owner's direction named: an EXPLICIT STACK in\nddg_build._walk_functions. Exactly equivalent for this function because there is\nno work after the child loop -- the recursion carried no state a worklist cannot\nhold and no post-order step -- and children are pushed REVERSED so pop() yields\nthem left to right, preserving the visit order that `out` accumulates in.\n\nRE-RAN THE ITEM'S OWN REPRO on the merged tree:\n    before   rc=1    0 bytes         RecursionError at ddg_build.py:206\n    after    rc=1    9,700 bytes     6 verdicts (2 violated, 4 inconclusive)\nThe EXIT CODE IS UNCHANGED in both, which is exactly the hazard this item was\nfiled for and exactly why a proxy metric would not have closed it: only the\nbyte count and the verdict list distinguish a crash from a finding.\n\nNON-DESTRUCTION ASSERTED, not assumed (L7). An A/B on poetry -- Python, so the\nDDG arm is actually active -- with arms differing only in this hunk, per-arm\nXDG_CACHE_HOME, and the arms instrument-checked first (arm A recursive, arm B\niterative): BYTE-IDENTICAL output.\n\nREGRESSION TEST: a 13KB fixture, in-package, at depths 2 / 1200 / 3000 so the\nproperty pinned is DEPTH-INDEPENDENCE rather than \"1200 works\". It also asserts\nthe function sitting AFTER the deep node is still analysed and that the deep and\nshallow arms yield the same analysed symbols and edge count -- because not\ncrashing is not enough: a walk that silently dropped its remaining work at the\ndeep node would pass a bare no-exception test.\n\nTWO TRAPS THE TEST GUARDS, both of which make a test pass VACUOUSLY:\n  * _DDG_LANGUAGES holds only python until the force-imports inside\n    _build_ddg_for_verify_claims run. This is what briefly led me to conclude\n    the crashing file was Python -- and keda contains ZERO .py files, which is\n    what exposed the mistake.\n  * The def/use extractor registers on IMPORT from a language package, and\n    test_cfg.py calls clear_def_use_extractors(), after which a bare import is a\n    no-op. Without the reload guard, ddg_edges is empty and every assertion\n    holds against a pipeline that computed nothing.\n\nWHAT IS NOT CLOSED, and it is deliberately left open rather than folded in. The\nSHAPE is a class: an AST sweep finds 72 self-recursive child-loop walks across\ncore and all three language packages. The corpus census says the realized\nexposure is narrow -- keda has 7 files at depth >= 900, all generated Go under\nvendor/, six of them golang.org/x/text/unicode/norm/tables*.go at 947-948, i.e.\nWITHIN 5% OF THE LIMIT; dash.js / caddy / mitmproxy top out at 74 / 37 / 79 --\nand `survey` over the same input does not crash while the Go analyzer parses the\noffending file fine (3,551 symbols). So the DDG walk was the single live\noffender and the other 71 have no measured failure. Rewriting all of them would\nbe a large diff across three packages justified by nothing measured; the census\nabove is the number a future reader should re-run before deciding otherwise.\n\nADJACENT, FILED AS SCOPE RATHER THAN CHANGED: _SKIP_DIRS excludes node_modules\nand .venv but NOT vendor/, which is why Go's vendored tree is walked at all.\nWhether hypergumbo should analyse vendored dependencies is a product question,\nnot a crash fix, so it is named here and left alone."  # 9d74
